WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.791
<v Tutor>Welcome to Part Two Daily.

2
00:00:01.791 --> 00:00:05.635
<v Tutor>It's Wednesday the fourth of November, and this is the deep dive.

3
00:00:05.635 --> 00:00:08.531
<v Tutor>Today is the high anion gap metabolic acidosis.

4
00:00:08.531 --> 00:00:10.374
<v Tutor>Not the whole of acid-base.

5
00:00:10.374 --> 00:00:17.958
<v Tutor>Just the gap: how to measure it properly, what fills it, and how to find the one cause that matters before the patient runs out of time.

6
00:00:17.958 --> 00:00:19.748
<v Tutor>Why this, in cardiac week?

7
00:00:19.748 --> 00:00:27.068
<v Tutor>Because today's practice set leans hard on metabolic acidosis, and because the theme is one of the most examined in the whole exam.

8
00:00:27.068 --> 00:00:33.440
<v Tutor>The cardiac links are real, too: the lactate after bypass, the lactate after adrenaline, the gas after an arrest.

9
00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:35.178
<v Tutor>Here's the running order.

10
00:00:35.178 --> 00:00:45.605
<v Tutor>First, a tutorial built on Oh's chapter fifty-seven, with toxic alcohols from chapter one hundred and one, and the governing guidance, the Surviving Sepsis Campaign and the EXTRIP workgroup.

11
00:00:45.605 --> 00:00:47.817
<v Tutor>Second, two trials on bicarbonate.

12
00:00:47.817 --> 00:00:52.399
<v Tutor>Third, exam practice with a real College question, an examiner and a candidate.

13
00:00:52.399 --> 00:00:53.505
<v Tutor>Pencil ready.

14
00:00:53.505 --> 00:00:54.400
<v Tutor>Let's go.

15
00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:57.309
<v Tutor>Start with a scenario.

16
00:00:57.309 --> 00:00:58.768
<v Tutor>Day three in the ICU.

17
00:00:58.768 --> 00:01:05.157
<v Tutor>A seventy-year-old woman, thin, treated for a wound infection with flucloxacillin, and on regular paracetamol for pain.

18
00:01:05.157 --> 00:01:06.615
<v Tutor>She's breathing fast.

19
00:01:06.615 --> 00:01:08.225
<v Tutor>Her bicarbonate is five.

20
00:01:08.225 --> 00:01:09.583
<v Tutor>Her lactate is one.

21
00:01:09.583 --> 00:01:11.193
<v Tutor>Her ketones are trivial.

22
00:01:11.193 --> 00:01:14.513
<v Tutor>Her creatinine is up, but not enough to explain any of it.

23
00:01:14.513 --> 00:01:17.079
<v Tutor>The registrar says, it must be the kidneys.

24
00:01:17.079 --> 00:01:17.783
<v Tutor>Is it?

25
00:01:17.783 --> 00:01:19.091
<v Tutor>Hold that thought.

26
00:01:19.091 --> 00:01:23.920
<v Tutor>By the end of this part you should be able to name the acid, the mechanism, and the fix.

27
00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:26.758
<v Tutor>First, how the College asks this.

28
00:01:26.758 --> 00:01:37.464
<v Tutor>In the curriculum's bank of past questions, causes of metabolic acidosis appears in eighteen past questions, from the 2011 second sitting through to the 2022 first sitting.

29
00:01:37.464 --> 00:01:40.675
<v Tutor>Causes of lactic acidosis appears in thirteen.

30
00:01:40.675 --> 00:01:49.180
<v Tutor>Pyroglutamic acidosis, which candidates still treat as a curiosity, has been examined five times, from 2011 to the 2025 second sitting.

31
00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:56.733
<v Tutor>And across the whole bank, sixty-three question parts have acidosis or the anion gap in their title, from 2007 to 2026.

32
00:01:56.733 --> 00:01:59.291
<v Tutor>The form is almost always the same.

33
00:01:59.291 --> 00:02:00.896
<v Tutor>A table of results.

34
00:02:00.896 --> 00:02:09.579
<v Tutor>Then three verbs: describe the abnormalities, list the likely causes, and give one associated clinical scenario, or explain the mechanism.

35
00:02:09.579 --> 00:02:12.375
<v Tutor>That means the marks sit in two places.

36
00:02:12.375 --> 00:02:15.289
<v Tutor>The numbers, written down and calculated.

37
00:02:15.289 --> 00:02:18.560
<v Tutor>And the specific cause, named, not gestured at.

38
00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:22.148
<v Tutor>So here is the framework the examiners reward.

39
00:02:22.148 --> 00:02:28.585
<v Tutor>Write it as a short stack of lines, one number per line, before you write a single word of diagnosis.

40
00:02:28.585 --> 00:02:29.588
<v Tutor>Line one.

41
00:02:29.588 --> 00:02:30.474
<v Tutor>The pH.

42
00:02:30.474 --> 00:02:32.363
<v Tutor>Acidaemia or alkalaemia.

43
00:02:32.363 --> 00:02:33.367
<v Tutor>Line two.

44
00:02:33.367 --> 00:02:35.021
<v Tutor>The primary process.

45
00:02:35.021 --> 00:02:38.977
<v Tutor>A low bicarbonate with a low pH means a metabolic acidosis.

46
00:02:38.977 --> 00:02:40.099
<v Tutor>Line three.

47
00:02:40.099 --> 00:02:41.339
<v Tutor>Compensation.

48
00:02:41.339 --> 00:02:50.963
<v Tutor>Winters' formula, from 1967: expected carbon dioxide equals one and a half times the bicarbonate, plus eight, plus or minus two, in millimetres of mercury.

49
00:02:50.963 --> 00:02:56.986
<v Tutor>If the measured carbon dioxide is higher than that, there's a coexisting respiratory acidosis.

50
00:02:56.986 --> 00:03:00.410
<v Tutor>If it's lower, a coexisting respiratory alkalosis.

51
00:03:00.410 --> 00:03:01.473
<v Tutor>Line four.

52
00:03:01.473 --> 00:03:02.772
<v Tutor>The anion gap.

53
00:03:02.772 --> 00:03:06.138
<v Tutor>Sodium minus the sum of chloride and bicarbonate.

54
00:03:06.138 --> 00:03:10.862
<v Tutor>The College convention leaves potassium out, and takes twelve as normal.

55
00:03:10.862 --> 00:03:11.924
<v Tutor>Line five.

56
00:03:11.924 --> 00:03:16.412
<v Tutor>Correct for albumin whenever albumin is below forty grams per litre.

57
00:03:16.412 --> 00:03:22.671
<v Tutor>Add a quarter of a millimole per litre to the gap for every gram per litre of albumin below forty.

58
00:03:22.671 --> 00:03:25.151
<v Tutor>So an albumin of twenty adds five.

59
00:03:25.151 --> 00:03:26.155
<v Tutor>Line six.

60
00:03:26.155 --> 00:03:27.572
<v Tutor>The delta ratio.

61
00:03:27.572 --> 00:03:30.996
<v Tutor>The rise in the gap, over the fall in bicarbonate.

62
00:03:30.996 --> 00:03:35.838
<v Tutor>That's the gap minus twelve, divided by twenty-four minus the bicarbonate.

63
00:03:35.838 --> 00:03:36.960
<v Tutor>Line seven.

64
00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:41.057
<v Tutor>If the gap is high and the cause isn't obvious, the osmolar gap.

65
00:03:41.057 --> 00:03:48.510
<v Tutor>Measured osmolality minus calculated, where calculated is two times sodium, plus glucose, plus urea, all in millimoles per litre.

66
00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:49.598
<v Tutor>Seven lines.

67
00:03:49.598 --> 00:03:52.046
<v Tutor>Perhaps ninety seconds of arithmetic.

68
00:03:52.046 --> 00:04:02.655
<v Tutor>In your own marked answers this month, this is exactly where the missing marks were: an expected carbon dioxide not calculated, a delta ratio not written, an osmolar gap left in the stem.

69
00:04:02.655 --> 00:04:04.940
<v Tutor>The numbers come before the words.

70
00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:06.242
<v Tutor>Try one.

71
00:04:06.242 --> 00:04:14.076
<v Tutor>A patient's sodium is one hundred and thirty-six, chloride one hundred and two, bicarbonate twenty, albumin twenty grams per litre.

72
00:04:14.076 --> 00:04:18.755
<v Tutor>What is the albumin-corrected anion gap, and does it change your diagnosis?

73
00:04:18.755 --> 00:04:20.220
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

74
00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:30.261
<v Tutor>The raw gap is one hundred and thirty-six minus one hundred and twenty-two.

75
00:04:30.261 --> 00:04:31.604
<v Tutor>That's fourteen.

76
00:04:31.604 --> 00:04:33.058
<v Tutor>Just above normal.

77
00:04:33.058 --> 00:04:34.344
<v Tutor>Now correct it.

78
00:04:34.344 --> 00:04:37.196
<v Tutor>Albumin is twenty below forty, so add five.

79
00:04:37.196 --> 00:04:39.321
<v Tutor>The corrected gap is nineteen.

80
00:04:39.321 --> 00:04:43.850
<v Tutor>That's a real high anion gap acidosis hiding behind a near-normal number.

81
00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:50.617
<v Tutor>This is the commonest trap in ICU acid-base, because our patients' albumin is so often in the teens and twenties.

82
00:04:50.617 --> 00:04:54.140
<v Tutor>An uncorrected gap in the ICU is a gap you can't trust.

83
00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:59.407
<v Tutor>Now the delta ratio, because it earns marks and candidates fumble the bands.

84
00:04:59.407 --> 00:05:04.738
<v Tutor>Below zero point four: the fall in bicarbonate is mostly not from unmeasured anions.

85
00:05:04.738 --> 00:05:08.156
<v Tutor>That's a hyperchloraemic normal anion gap acidosis.

86
00:05:08.156 --> 00:05:13.197
<v Tutor>Zero point four to zero point eight: a mixed picture, high gap plus normal gap.

87
00:05:13.197 --> 00:05:17.716
<v Tutor>Think renal failure, or a ketoacidosis being resuscitated with saline.

88
00:05:17.716 --> 00:05:20.324
<v Tutor>One to two: a pure high gap acidosis.

89
00:05:20.324 --> 00:05:25.017
<v Tutor>Ketoacidosis tends to sit near one, because ketoanions are lost in urine.

90
00:05:25.017 --> 00:05:32.955
<v Tutor>Lactic acidosis tends to sit around one point six, partly because lactate leaves cells more slowly than hydrogen ion is buffered.

91
00:05:32.955 --> 00:05:37.300
<v Tutor>Above two: the bicarbonate is higher than it should be for the gap.

92
00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:45.991
<v Tutor>That means a coexisting metabolic alkalosis, from vomiting, diuretics or nasogastric loss, or a pre-existing compensated respiratory acidosis.

93
00:05:45.991 --> 00:05:48.077
<v Tutor>Say which one fits the stem.

94
00:05:48.077 --> 00:05:51.380
<v Tutor>Examiners want the inference, not just the ratio.

95
00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:53.918
<v Tutor>Now, what fills the gap.

96
00:05:53.918 --> 00:05:56.123
<v Tutor>You'll remember MUDPILES.

97
00:05:56.123 --> 00:06:02.872
<v Tutor>It dates from an era of paraldehyde, and it misses two causes that ICU patients actually get.

98
00:06:02.872 --> 00:06:08.886
<v Tutor>So in 2008 Mehta and the Emmetts wrote a letter to the Lancet proposing GOLD MARK.

99
00:06:08.886 --> 00:06:12.694
<v Tutor>G, glycols: ethylene glycol and propylene glycol.

100
00:06:12.694 --> 00:06:16.035
<v Tutor>O, oxoproline, which is pyroglutamic acid.

101
00:06:16.035 --> 00:06:17.438
<v Tutor>L, L-lactate.

102
00:06:17.438 --> 00:06:18.842
<v Tutor>D, D-lactate.

103
00:06:18.842 --> 00:06:20.178
<v Tutor>M, methanol.

104
00:06:20.178 --> 00:06:21.447
<v Tutor>A, aspirin.

105
00:06:21.447 --> 00:06:23.118
<v Tutor>R, renal failure.

106
00:06:23.118 --> 00:06:24.722
<v Tutor>K, ketoacidosis.

107
00:06:24.722 --> 00:06:28.063
<v Tutor>The value of GOLD MARK isn't the mnemonic.

108
00:06:28.063 --> 00:06:32.272
<v Tutor>It's that it forces two questions MUDPILES never asked.

109
00:06:32.272 --> 00:06:34.477
<v Tutor>Could this be oxoproline?

110
00:06:34.477 --> 00:06:38.353
<v Tutor>And could the lactate assay be blind to this acid?

111
00:06:38.353 --> 00:06:45.034
<v Tutor>In an SAQ, list them in order of likelihood for the patient in the stem, not alphabetically.

112
00:06:45.034 --> 00:06:50.113
<v Tutor>A post-operative cardiac patient on adrenaline gets L-lactate first.

113
00:06:50.113 --> 00:06:56.260
<v Tutor>A malnourished woman on flucloxacillin and paracetamol gets oxoproline near the top.

114
00:06:56.660 --> 00:07:01.128
<v Tutor>Take lactate first, because it's the cause you'll see every day.

115
00:07:01.128 --> 00:07:08.824
<v Tutor>Oh's chapter fifty-seven treats it under hyperlactataemia, and the useful split is still Cohen and Woods, from 1976.

116
00:07:08.824 --> 00:07:14.906
<v Tutor>Type A is the hypoxic kind: shock, regional ischaemia, severe hypoxaemia, carbon monoxide.

117
00:07:14.906 --> 00:07:17.947
<v Tutor>Type B is lactate without global hypoxia.

118
00:07:17.947 --> 00:07:23.160
<v Tutor>B one, with disease: sepsis, liver failure, malignancy, thiamine deficiency.

119
00:07:23.160 --> 00:07:32.593
<v Tutor>B two, from drugs and toxins: adrenaline, salbutamol, metformin, linezolid, propofol infusion syndrome, cyanide, the nucleoside antiretrovirals.

120
00:07:32.593 --> 00:07:34.766
<v Tutor>B three, the inborn errors.

121
00:07:34.766 --> 00:07:37.682
<v Tutor>The cardiac links this week are direct.

122
00:07:37.682 --> 00:07:45.130
<v Tutor>After bypass, lactate rises from low flow, from hypothermia, from lactate-containing prime, and from adrenaline.

123
00:07:45.130 --> 00:07:49.784
<v Tutor>Adrenaline is worth a sentence of mechanism, because it's examined.

124
00:07:49.784 --> 00:07:56.239
<v Tutor>It drives beta-two stimulated aerobic glycolysis and sodium-potassium ATPase activity in muscle.

125
00:07:56.239 --> 00:08:03.128
<v Tutor>Pyruvate is made faster than the mitochondria can take it, so lactate rises with no oxygen debt at all.

126
00:08:03.128 --> 00:08:13.740
<v Tutor>That's why a rising lactate on adrenaline with a good cardiac output and a falling central venous to arterial carbon dioxide gap isn't a signal to give more fluid.

127
00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:25.269
<v Tutor>The Controversies in Critical Care chapter on hyperlactataemia makes the larger point: lactate is a fuel and a signal, not just a waste product, and clearance is a marker, not a target in itself.

128
00:08:25.269 --> 00:08:29.127
<v Tutor>The Surviving Sepsis Campaign's 2026 guideline reflects that.

129
00:08:29.127 --> 00:08:37.960
<v Tutor>It suggests measuring lactate, and serial lactate to guide resuscitation, but its remarks warn against continuing fluids just to normalise the number.

130
00:08:38.360 --> 00:08:39.906
<v Tutor>Two lactate traps.

131
00:08:39.906 --> 00:08:41.393
<v Tutor>First, D-lactate.

132
00:08:41.393 --> 00:08:44.188
<v Tutor>Standard assays measure L-lactate only.

133
00:08:44.188 --> 00:08:51.384
<v Tutor>So the patient with short bowel syndrome, confused after a carbohydrate load, has a big gap and a normal lactate.

134
00:08:51.384 --> 00:08:54.417
<v Tutor>The gap is D-lactate from colonic bacteria.

135
00:08:54.417 --> 00:08:55.963
<v Tutor>Second, metformin.

136
00:08:55.963 --> 00:09:00.661
<v Tutor>Metformin-associated lactic acidosis can push the lactate above twenty.

137
00:09:00.661 --> 00:09:13.209
<v Tutor>The EXTRIP workgroup, Calello and colleagues in 2015, recommend extracorporeal removal if the lactate is above twenty millimoles per litre, the pH is seven point zero or lower, or standard therapy fails.

138
00:09:13.209 --> 00:09:18.145
<v Tutor>Shock, kidney impairment and a reduced conscious state lower the threshold.

139
00:09:18.145 --> 00:09:23.497
<v Tutor>Intermittent haemodialysis with a bicarbonate bath is the preferred first session.

140
00:09:23.497 --> 00:09:29.920
<v Tutor>Stop when the lactate is under three and the pH above seven point three five, and watch for rebound.

141
00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:32.443
<v Tutor>Ketoacidosis next, briefly.

142
00:09:32.443 --> 00:09:35.051
<v Tutor>Diabetic, alcoholic and starvation.

143
00:09:35.051 --> 00:09:36.506
<v Tutor>Two exam points.

144
00:09:36.506 --> 00:09:37.234
<v Tutor>One.

145
00:09:37.234 --> 00:09:43.117
<v Tutor>The euglycaemic diabetic ketoacidosis of the sodium-glucose cotransporter two inhibitors.

146
00:09:43.117 --> 00:09:45.785
<v Tutor>A normal glucose doesn't exclude it.

147
00:09:45.785 --> 00:09:48.029
<v Tutor>Measure beta-hydroxybutyrate.

148
00:09:48.029 --> 00:09:48.757
<v Tutor>Two.

149
00:09:48.757 --> 00:09:54.700
<v Tutor>The nitroprusside test on a urine dipstick detects acetoacetate, not beta-hydroxybutyrate.

150
00:09:54.700 --> 00:10:05.496
<v Tutor>In alcoholic ketoacidosis, the redox state pushes almost everything to beta-hydroxybutyrate, so the dipstick can look reassuring while the patient is profoundly acidotic.

151
00:10:05.496 --> 00:10:09.317
<v Tutor>Renal failure: early on it gives a normal gap acidosis.

152
00:10:09.317 --> 00:10:16.534
<v Tutor>The gap rises only when the glomerular filtration rate is very low, as sulfate, phosphate and urate accumulate.

153
00:10:16.534 --> 00:10:23.326
<v Tutor>So when a modest creatinine rise is offered as the explanation for a bicarbonate of five, be suspicious.

154
00:10:23.326 --> 00:10:26.480
<v Tutor>That's the scenario at the top of this part.

155
00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:29.274
<v Tutor>Which brings us to pyroglutamic acid.

156
00:10:29.274 --> 00:10:31.456
<v Tutor>It's also called five-oxoproline.

157
00:10:31.456 --> 00:10:40.289
<v Tutor>And it's where this deep dive earns its keep, because you scored two out of ten on this on the nineteenth of October, after four and a half on the fourteenth.

158
00:10:40.289 --> 00:10:43.641
<v Tutor>On the nineteenth, the gap was put down to the kidneys.

159
00:10:43.641 --> 00:10:48.111
<v Tutor>Here's the mechanism, slowly, because the College answer gives marks for it.

160
00:10:48.111 --> 00:10:50.292
<v Tutor>Glutathione is made in two steps.

161
00:10:50.292 --> 00:10:54.496
<v Tutor>Glutamate and cysteine are joined by gamma-glutamylcysteine synthetase.

162
00:10:54.496 --> 00:10:57.476
<v Tutor>Then glycine is added by glutathione synthetase.

163
00:10:57.476 --> 00:11:00.881
<v Tutor>The first enzyme is held in check by glutathione itself.

164
00:11:00.881 --> 00:11:02.264
<v Tutor>Negative feedback.

165
00:11:02.264 --> 00:11:03.967
<v Tutor>Now deplete glutathione.

166
00:11:03.967 --> 00:11:07.372
<v Tutor>Paracetamol does it, through its toxic metabolite NAPQI.

167
00:11:07.372 --> 00:11:10.405
<v Tutor>So do sepsis, malnutrition and liver dysfunction.

168
00:11:10.405 --> 00:11:11.895
<v Tutor>The brake comes off.

169
00:11:11.895 --> 00:11:19.717
<v Tutor>Gamma-glutamylcysteine is made in excess, and when glycine is short, it's diverted by gamma-glutamyl cyclotransferase into five-oxoproline.

170
00:11:19.717 --> 00:11:25.251
<v Tutor>Normally five-oxoprolinase, an ATP-dependent enzyme, converts five-oxoproline back to glutamate.

171
00:11:25.251 --> 00:11:27.911
<v Tutor>Flucloxacillin inhibits five-oxoprolinase.

172
00:11:27.911 --> 00:11:30.199
<v Tutor>So does netilmicin, and vigabatrin.

173
00:11:30.199 --> 00:11:31.742
<v Tutor>So you have two hits.

174
00:11:31.742 --> 00:11:33.552
<v Tutor>Paracetamol opens the tap.

175
00:11:33.552 --> 00:11:35.680
<v Tutor>Flucloxacillin blocks the drain.

176
00:11:35.860 --> 00:11:44.474
<v Tutor>The typical patient: female, elderly, malnourished, septic, with renal or hepatic impairment, on regular paracetamol, and often on flucloxacillin.

177
00:11:44.474 --> 00:11:47.775
<v Tutor>Diagnosis is by exclusion first, then confirmation.

178
00:11:47.775 --> 00:11:48.837
<v Tutor>A high gap.

179
00:11:48.837 --> 00:11:51.187
<v Tutor>A lactate that doesn't explain it.

180
00:11:51.187 --> 00:11:53.312
<v Tutor>Ketones that don't explain it.

181
00:11:53.312 --> 00:11:56.892
<v Tutor>No toxic alcohol, no salicylate, kidneys not bad enough.

182
00:11:56.892 --> 00:12:00.193
<v Tutor>Then urinary organic acids showing five-oxoproline.

183
00:12:00.193 --> 00:12:01.871
<v Tutor>Treatment is specific.

184
00:12:01.871 --> 00:12:03.269
<v Tutor>Stop paracetamol.

185
00:12:03.269 --> 00:12:05.395
<v Tutor>Stop or change flucloxacillin.

186
00:12:05.395 --> 00:12:06.793
<v Tutor>Treat the sepsis.

187
00:12:06.793 --> 00:12:08.192
<v Tutor>Feed the patient.

188
00:12:08.192 --> 00:12:11.268
<v Tutor>Give N-acetylcysteine to replenish glutathione.

189
00:12:11.268 --> 00:12:15.519
<v Tutor>Bicarbonate and dialysis may support, but they aren't the treatment.

190
00:12:15.519 --> 00:12:25.700
<v Tutor>In the College answer to the 2014 second-sitting question, the management line reads: supportive care, cease culprit drugs, treat sepsis, improve nutrition, N-acetylcysteine.

191
00:12:26.100 --> 00:12:31.929
<v Tutor>Name the two drugs that cause pyroglutamic acidosis most often, and the step each one hits.

192
00:12:31.929 --> 00:12:33.460
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

193
00:12:38.860 --> 00:12:48.162
<v Tutor>Paracetamol depletes glutathione, which removes the negative feedback on gamma-glutamylcysteine synthetase, so excess substrate is cycled into five-oxoproline.

194
00:12:48.162 --> 00:12:54.011
<v Tutor>Flucloxacillin inhibits five-oxoprolinase, so five-oxoproline can't be cleared back to glutamate.

195
00:12:54.011 --> 00:12:55.794
<v Tutor>Tap open, drain blocked.

196
00:12:55.794 --> 00:12:59.860
<v Tutor>If you write those two sentences, you've got the mechanism marks.

197
00:13:00.260 --> 00:13:06.377
<v Tutor>Now a story, because the toxic alcohols have a history that shaped how every drug you prescribe is regulated.

198
00:13:06.377 --> 00:13:10.977
<v Tutor>In 1937, sulfanilamide was the new wonder drug, one of the first antibacterials.

199
00:13:10.977 --> 00:13:14.898
<v Tutor>It came as tablets, and there was demand for a liquid for children.

200
00:13:14.898 --> 00:13:16.676
<v Tutor>A company in Tennessee, S.

201
00:13:16.676 --> 00:13:17.198
<v Tutor>E.

202
00:13:17.198 --> 00:13:21.538
<v Tutor>Massengill, found that sulfanilamide dissolved nicely in diethylene glycol.

203
00:13:21.538 --> 00:13:23.158
<v Tutor>It tasted of raspberry.

204
00:13:23.158 --> 00:13:27.602
<v Tutor>They called it Elixir Sulfanilamide, and shipped it across the United States.

205
00:13:27.602 --> 00:13:31.105
<v Tutor>Diethylene glycol is a close chemical cousin of antifreeze.

206
00:13:31.105 --> 00:13:34.398
<v Tutor>Within weeks people were dying of acute kidney failure.

207
00:13:34.398 --> 00:13:37.326
<v Tutor>More than a hundred died, many of them children.

208
00:13:37.326 --> 00:13:38.633
<v Tutor>Here's the twist.

209
00:13:38.633 --> 00:13:42.397
<v Tutor>There was no law requiring that a new drug be tested for safety.

210
00:13:42.397 --> 00:13:49.612
<v Tutor>The federal inspectors could only act on a technicality: an elixir, by definition, contained alcohol, and this one contained none.

211
00:13:49.612 --> 00:13:51.598
<v Tutor>So the charge was misbranding.

212
00:13:51.598 --> 00:13:57.244
<v Tutor>One of the scientists who helped confirm that the solvent was the killer was a young Frances Kelsey.

213
00:13:57.244 --> 00:14:02.420
<v Tutor>Two decades later, at the Food and Drug Administration, she refused to approve thalidomide.

214
00:14:02.600 --> 00:14:11.215
<v Tutor>The public anger produced the Federal Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act of 1938, which for the first time required proof of safety before a drug could be sold.

215
00:14:11.215 --> 00:14:12.651
<v Tutor>And the exam point?

216
00:14:12.651 --> 00:14:16.693
<v Tutor>The glycols kill through their metabolites, not the parent compound.

217
00:14:16.693 --> 00:14:18.926
<v Tutor>The parent raises the osmolar gap.

218
00:14:18.926 --> 00:14:22.383
<v Tutor>The metabolites raise the anion gap and wreck the kidney.

219
00:14:22.383 --> 00:14:25.680
<v Tutor>That pattern is the whole of toxic alcohol management.

220
00:14:26.080 --> 00:14:30.640
<v Tutor>So, the toxic alcohols, as Oh's chapter one hundred and one sets them out.

221
00:14:30.640 --> 00:14:31.585
<v Tutor>Methanol.

222
00:14:31.585 --> 00:14:34.310
<v Tutor>Alcohol dehydrogenase makes formaldehyde.

223
00:14:34.310 --> 00:14:37.035
<v Tutor>Aldehyde dehydrogenase makes formic acid.

224
00:14:37.035 --> 00:14:38.647
<v Tutor>Formate is the toxin.

225
00:14:38.647 --> 00:14:45.265
<v Tutor>It inhibits cytochrome c oxidase, so it adds a lactic acidosis, and it targets the optic nerve and the putamen.

226
00:14:45.265 --> 00:14:56.164
<v Tutor>So the picture is a high anion gap acidosis, a raised osmolar gap early, visual blurring or a snowfield in the vision, and in severe cases basal ganglia haemorrhage or necrosis on imaging.

227
00:14:56.164 --> 00:15:01.280
<v Tutor>Formate is cleared by a folate-dependent pathway, which is why we give folinic acid.

228
00:15:01.280 --> 00:15:02.614
<v Tutor>Ethylene glycol.

229
00:15:02.614 --> 00:15:06.507
<v Tutor>Alcohol dehydrogenase makes glycoaldehyde, then glycolic acid.

230
00:15:06.507 --> 00:15:08.453
<v Tutor>Glycolate is the main acid.

231
00:15:08.453 --> 00:15:11.956
<v Tutor>It's then metabolised to glyoxylate, and on to oxalate.

232
00:15:11.956 --> 00:15:13.624
<v Tutor>Oxalate binds calcium.

233
00:15:13.624 --> 00:15:19.018
<v Tutor>So you get calcium oxalate crystals in the urine, hypocalcaemia, and acute kidney injury.

234
00:15:19.018 --> 00:15:25.024
<v Tutor>Thiamine and pyridoxine are cofactors that steer glyoxylate away from oxalate, so they're given too.

235
00:15:25.024 --> 00:15:28.305
<v Tutor>And the gaps move in opposite directions over time.

236
00:15:28.305 --> 00:15:34.255
<v Tutor>Early, the parent alcohol is in the blood: the osmolar gap is high and the anion gap may be normal.

237
00:15:34.255 --> 00:15:39.760
<v Tutor>Late, the parent is metabolised: the anion gap is high and the osmolar gap may have closed.

238
00:15:39.940 --> 00:15:43.566
<v Tutor>A normal osmolar gap doesn't exclude a late presentation.

239
00:15:43.566 --> 00:15:46.913
<v Tutor>Remember that ethanol itself raises the osmolar gap.

240
00:15:46.913 --> 00:15:53.216
<v Tutor>Each millimole per litre of ethanol adds roughly one to the gap; some use a factor of one point two five.

241
00:15:53.216 --> 00:15:56.340
<v Tutor>Account for it before you blame a toxic alcohol.

242
00:15:56.740 --> 00:16:00.646
<v Tutor>A patient presents eighteen hours after drinking antifreeze.

243
00:16:00.646 --> 00:16:02.829
<v Tutor>The anion gap is twenty-eight.

244
00:16:02.829 --> 00:16:04.610
<v Tutor>The osmolar gap is six.

245
00:16:04.610 --> 00:16:08.286
<v Tutor>Why is the osmolar gap normal, and does it reassure you?

246
00:16:08.286 --> 00:16:09.780
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

247
00:16:15.180 --> 00:16:19.843
<v Tutor>It's normal because most of the ethylene glycol has already been metabolised.

248
00:16:19.843 --> 00:16:26.262
<v Tutor>The osmotically active parent is gone, and its acid metabolites, glycolate above all, now fill the anion gap.

249
00:16:26.262 --> 00:16:28.401
<v Tutor>It doesn't reassure you at all.

250
00:16:28.401 --> 00:16:30.376
<v Tutor>This is the dangerous phase.

251
00:16:30.376 --> 00:16:36.740
<v Tutor>An anion gap above twenty-seven is itself a strong EXTRIP indication for dialysis, as we'll see in a moment.

252
00:16:37.140 --> 00:16:38.179
<v Tutor>Management.

253
00:16:38.179 --> 00:16:43.923
<v Tutor>First, block alcohol dehydrogenase, so the parent is excreted unchanged and no more acid is made.

254
00:16:43.923 --> 00:16:46.276
<v Tutor>Fomepizole is the cleaner antidote.

255
00:16:46.276 --> 00:16:57.983
<v Tutor>Fifteen milligrams per kilogram as a load, then ten milligrams per kilogram every twelve hours for four doses, then fifteen milligrams per kilogram every twelve hours, because it induces its own metabolism.

256
00:16:57.983 --> 00:17:02.086
<v Tutor>During haemodialysis it's dialysed, so it's given every four hours.

257
00:17:02.086 --> 00:17:06.189
<v Tutor>Ethanol is the older alternative and still the one on many shelves.

258
00:17:06.189 --> 00:17:10.729
<v Tutor>It has a much higher affinity for alcohol dehydrogenase than methanol does.

259
00:17:10.729 --> 00:17:17.184
<v Tutor>Aim for a blood ethanol of about twenty-two millimoles per litre, which is a hundred milligrams per decilitre.

260
00:17:17.184 --> 00:17:24.241
<v Tutor>It needs close monitoring, and it brings intoxication, hypoglycaemia and a messy infusion, often doubled during dialysis.

261
00:17:24.241 --> 00:17:28.454
<v Tutor>Availability of fomepizole varies, so know your local antidote stock.

262
00:17:28.454 --> 00:17:31.189
<v Tutor>The evidence base for fomepizole is small.

263
00:17:31.189 --> 00:17:37.371
<v Tutor>Brent and colleagues' 1999 New England Journal case series in ethylene glycol enrolled nineteen patients.

264
00:17:37.371 --> 00:17:44.865
<v Tutor>Glycolate fell once fomepizole started, and patients with a normal creatinine at the start didn't go on to develop kidney injury.

265
00:17:44.865 --> 00:17:46.780
<v Tutor>Second, give the cofactors.

266
00:17:46.960 --> 00:17:48.722
<v Tutor>Folinic acid for methanol.

267
00:17:48.722 --> 00:17:51.416
<v Tutor>Thiamine and pyridoxine for ethylene glycol.

268
00:17:51.416 --> 00:17:55.562
<v Tutor>Third, dialysis, which removes both the parent and the acid metabolites.

269
00:17:55.562 --> 00:17:58.360
<v Tutor>This is where the governing guidance comes in.

270
00:17:58.760 --> 00:18:01.665
<v Tutor>The EXTRIP workgroup is the guideline here.

271
00:18:01.665 --> 00:18:06.165
<v Tutor>For methanol, Roberts and colleagues in Critical Care Medicine in 2015.

272
00:18:06.165 --> 00:18:10.609
<v Tutor>For ethylene glycol, Ghannoum and colleagues in Critical Care in 2023.

273
00:18:10.609 --> 00:18:16.476
<v Tutor>Both rate the evidence as very low quality, and both are what an examiner expects you to quote.

274
00:18:16.476 --> 00:18:17.444
<v Tutor>Methanol.

275
00:18:17.444 --> 00:18:30.774
<v Tutor>Recommend extracorporeal treatment for severe poisoning: coma, seizures, new visual deficits, a pH of seven point one five or lower, persistent acidosis despite supportive care and antidotes, or an anion gap above twenty-four.

276
00:18:30.774 --> 00:18:44.446
<v Tutor>Also on the methanol concentration: above seven hundred milligrams per litre with fomepizole, which is about twenty-two millimoles per litre; above six hundred with ethanol; above five hundred with no alcohol dehydrogenase blockade.

277
00:18:44.446 --> 00:18:46.554
<v Tutor>And impaired kidney function.

278
00:18:46.554 --> 00:18:50.028
<v Tutor>Intermittent haemodialysis is the modality of choice.

279
00:18:50.028 --> 00:18:54.870
<v Tutor>Avoid systemic anticoagulation, because of the risk of putaminal haemorrhage.

280
00:18:54.870 --> 00:18:57.548
<v Tutor>Continue the antidote and folinic acid.

281
00:18:57.548 --> 00:19:03.130
<v Tutor>Stop when methanol is below two hundred milligrams per litre and the patient is improving.

282
00:19:03.130 --> 00:19:04.497
<v Tutor>Ethylene glycol.

283
00:19:04.497 --> 00:19:10.080
<v Tutor>Recommend dialysis for coma, seizures, or acute kidney injury at KDIGO stage two or three.

284
00:19:10.260 --> 00:19:15.878
<v Tutor>Recommend it for a glycolate above twelve millimoles per litre or an anion gap above twenty-seven.

285
00:19:15.878 --> 00:19:19.482
<v Tutor>Suggest it for an anion gap of twenty-three to twenty-seven.

286
00:19:19.482 --> 00:19:27.484
<v Tutor>If the patient is on fomepizole with an ethylene glycol above fifty millimoles per litre, or an osmolar gap above fifty, dialysis is suggested.

287
00:19:27.484 --> 00:19:30.452
<v Tutor>On ethanol at the same levels, it's recommended.

288
00:19:30.452 --> 00:19:33.102
<v Tutor>Stop when the anion gap is below eighteen.

289
00:19:33.102 --> 00:19:35.593
<v Tutor>Notice what that means for the bedside.

290
00:19:35.593 --> 00:19:41.317
<v Tutor>With fomepizole on board and good kidneys, a high ethylene glycol level alone may not need dialysis.

291
00:19:41.317 --> 00:19:44.020
<v Tutor>The acid and the kidney drive the decision.

292
00:19:44.420 --> 00:19:49.169
<v Tutor>A word on salicylate, because it's in GOLD MARK and it shows up as a mixed gas.

293
00:19:49.169 --> 00:19:54.738
<v Tutor>Salicylate stimulates the respiratory centre directly and uncouples oxidative phosphorylation.

294
00:19:54.738 --> 00:19:59.705
<v Tutor>So the classic adult gas is a respiratory alkalosis with a high anion gap acidosis.

295
00:19:59.705 --> 00:20:06.475
<v Tutor>When you see a carbon dioxide well below the Winters' prediction with a high gap, salicylate should be on your list.

296
00:20:06.475 --> 00:20:11.770
<v Tutor>And the cardiac link this week: in cardiac arrest, routine bicarbonate isn't recommended.

297
00:20:11.770 --> 00:20:15.100
<v Tutor>Keep it for hyperkalaemia and for tricyclic toxicity.

298
00:20:15.500 --> 00:20:17.917
<v Tutor>Where do candidates lose marks?

299
00:20:17.917 --> 00:20:22.192
<v Tutor>From the examiner comments and your own scripts, four places.

300
00:20:22.192 --> 00:20:22.936
<v Tutor>One.

301
00:20:22.936 --> 00:20:24.423
<v Tutor>Not calculating.

302
00:20:24.423 --> 00:20:29.071
<v Tutor>Writing high anion gap metabolic acidosis with no number beside it.

303
00:20:29.071 --> 00:20:29.814
<v Tutor>Two.

304
00:20:29.814 --> 00:20:33.594
<v Tutor>Not correcting the gap for albumin in an ICU patient.

305
00:20:33.594 --> 00:20:34.462
<v Tutor>Three.

306
00:20:34.462 --> 00:20:37.250
<v Tutor>Naming a category instead of a cause.

307
00:20:37.250 --> 00:20:42.394
<v Tutor>Toxic alcohol earns less than ethylene glycol, with the reason in the data.

308
00:20:42.394 --> 00:20:51.627
<v Tutor>In the 2020 second-sitting question, which you scored five and a half on, ethylene glycol and the glycolate mechanism were the missing marks.

309
00:20:51.627 --> 00:20:52.432
<v Tutor>Four.

310
00:20:52.432 --> 00:20:55.159
<v Tutor>Leaving the compensation unexamined.

311
00:20:55.159 --> 00:21:01.665
<v Tutor>An inadequate or excessive respiratory response is a second diagnosis, and a second set of marks.

312
00:21:01.665 --> 00:21:03.772
<v Tutor>Three numbers to remember.

313
00:21:03.772 --> 00:21:09.783
<v Tutor>Expected carbon dioxide: one and a half times bicarbonate, plus eight, plus or minus two.

314
00:21:09.783 --> 00:21:14.307
<v Tutor>Albumin correction: add a quarter per gram per litre below forty.

315
00:21:14.307 --> 00:21:19.822
<v Tutor>And EXTRIP for ethylene glycol: dialyse when the anion gap is above twenty-seven.

316
00:21:19.822 --> 00:21:23.850
<v Tutor>So, the woman at the start, with the bicarbonate of five.

317
00:21:23.850 --> 00:21:25.709
<v Tutor>Pyroglutamic acidosis.

318
00:21:25.709 --> 00:21:29.984
<v Tutor>Paracetamol opened the tap, flucloxacillin blocked the drain.

319
00:21:29.984 --> 00:21:34.260
<v Tutor>Stop both, feed her, treat the sepsis, give N-acetylcysteine.

320
00:21:35.660 --> 00:21:39.752
<v Tutor>Here's a question that has divided intensivists for fifty years.

321
00:21:39.752 --> 00:21:42.708
<v Tutor>If the blood is acid, should we give alkali?

322
00:21:42.708 --> 00:21:44.640
<v Tutor>The case for is intuitive.

323
00:21:44.640 --> 00:21:51.631
<v Tutor>Severe acidaemia depresses myocardial contractility, blunts the response to catecholamines, and causes arrhythmias.

324
00:21:51.631 --> 00:21:54.018
<v Tutor>The case against is physiological.

325
00:21:54.018 --> 00:21:57.770
<v Tutor>Bicarbonate buffers hydrogen ion by making carbon dioxide.

326
00:21:57.770 --> 00:22:06.579
<v Tutor>Carbon dioxide crosses cell membranes faster than bicarbonate does, so in a patient who can't ventilate it off, intracellular pH can actually fall.

327
00:22:06.579 --> 00:22:11.070
<v Tutor>Bicarbonate also lowers ionised calcium, loads sodium, and adds volume.

328
00:22:11.070 --> 00:22:18.345
<v Tutor>Eight point four percent bicarbonate is one millimole per millilitre and roughly two thousand milliosmoles per kilogram.

329
00:22:18.345 --> 00:22:22.323
<v Tutor>For decades, the argument ran on physiology and small studies.

330
00:22:22.323 --> 00:22:24.540
<v Tutor>Then the French ran two trials.

331
00:22:24.940 --> 00:22:25.969
<v Tutor>BICAR-ICU.

332
00:22:25.969 --> 00:22:28.656
<v Tutor>Jaber and colleagues, the Lancet, 2018.

333
00:22:28.656 --> 00:22:30.429
<v Tutor>Twenty-six French ICUs.

334
00:22:30.429 --> 00:22:47.238
<v Tutor>Three hundred and eighty-nine adults within forty-eight hours of admission with severe metabolic acidaemia: pH seven point two or lower, carbon dioxide forty-five millimetres of mercury or lower, bicarbonate twenty or lower, and a SOFA score of four or more or a lactate of two or more.

335
00:22:47.238 --> 00:22:51.755
<v Tutor>Patients with gastrointestinal or renal bicarbonate loss were excluded.

336
00:22:51.755 --> 00:22:54.500
<v Tutor>This was about acid gain, mostly lactic.

337
00:22:54.500 --> 00:23:09.765
<v Tutor>The intervention was four point two percent sodium bicarbonate, one hundred and twenty-five to two hundred and fifty millilitres over thirty minutes, repeated to keep the pH above seven point three, up to a thousand millilitres in the first twenty-four hours.

338
00:23:09.765 --> 00:23:11.995
<v Tutor>The control was no bicarbonate.

339
00:23:11.995 --> 00:23:18.570
<v Tutor>The primary outcome was a composite: death by day twenty-eight, or at least one organ failure at day seven.

340
00:23:18.570 --> 00:23:24.288
<v Tutor>It occurred in sixty-six percent with bicarbonate, against seventy-one percent with control.

341
00:23:24.288 --> 00:23:25.660
<v Tutor>Not significant.

342
00:23:25.840 --> 00:23:39.402
<v Tutor>But in the prespecified stratum with acute kidney injury, AKIN stage two or three, around one hundred and eighty patients, day twenty-eight survival was fifty-four percent with bicarbonate against thirty-seven percent with control.

343
00:23:39.402 --> 00:23:44.282
<v Tutor>Overall, renal replacement therapy fell from fifty-two to thirty-five percent.

344
00:23:44.282 --> 00:23:53.191
<v Tutor>The harms were what physiology predicts: more metabolic alkalosis, hypernatraemia and hypocalcaemia, with no life-threatening complications reported.

345
00:23:53.191 --> 00:24:02.043
<v Tutor>The criticisms: open label, no placebo infusion, about a quarter of controls received bicarbonate anyway, and the striking result was in a subgroup.

346
00:24:02.043 --> 00:24:04.880
<v Tutor>A subgroup is a hypothesis, not an answer.

347
00:24:05.280 --> 00:24:08.027
<v Tutor>And that hypothesis was tested directly.

348
00:24:08.027 --> 00:24:09.114
<v Tutor>BICARICU-2.

349
00:24:09.114 --> 00:24:11.403
<v Tutor>Jung and colleagues, JAMA, 2025.

350
00:24:11.403 --> 00:24:13.234
<v Tutor>Forty-three French ICUs.

351
00:24:13.234 --> 00:24:18.327
<v Tutor>Six hundred and forty patients randomised, six hundred and twenty-seven analysed.

352
00:24:18.327 --> 00:24:28.113
<v Tutor>The same acidaemia criteria, confirmed on two gases at least thirty minutes apart, and this time everyone had to have KDIGO stage two or three acute kidney injury.

353
00:24:28.113 --> 00:24:34.122
<v Tutor>Ketoacidosis, gastrointestinal bicarbonate loss and an immediate need for dialysis were excluded.

354
00:24:34.122 --> 00:24:38.242
<v Tutor>Same bicarbonate regimen, to a pH of at least seven point three.

355
00:24:38.242 --> 00:24:41.275
<v Tutor>The primary outcome was ninety-day mortality.

356
00:24:41.275 --> 00:24:46.997
<v Tutor>Sixty-two point one percent with bicarbonate, sixty-one point seven percent with usual care.

357
00:24:46.997 --> 00:24:55.352
<v Tutor>A difference of zero point four percentage points, ninety-five percent confidence interval minus seven point two to plus eight point zero.

358
00:24:55.352 --> 00:24:56.783
<v Tutor>No signal at all.

359
00:24:56.783 --> 00:25:03.535
<v Tutor>Kidney replacement therapy, a secondary outcome, fell from fifty point two to thirty-four point seven percent.

360
00:25:03.535 --> 00:25:05.538
<v Tutor>The criticisms matter here.

361
00:25:05.538 --> 00:25:07.026
<v Tutor>It was open label.

362
00:25:07.026 --> 00:25:16.640
<v Tutor>Clinicians decided when to start dialysis, and severe acidaemia is itself a dialysis criterion, so correcting the pH may simply have removed one reason to dial.

363
00:25:16.820 --> 00:25:24.061
<v Tutor>The trial assumed a control mortality of eighty percent; it was about sixty-two, so it was underpowered for a modest effect.

364
00:25:24.061 --> 00:25:27.901
<v Tutor>And fifteen percent of the control group received bicarbonate.

365
00:25:27.901 --> 00:25:32.070
<v Tutor>There's a lesson in that sequence that goes well beyond bicarbonate.

366
00:25:32.070 --> 00:25:38.598
<v Tutor>A striking subgroup in a neutral trial, tested in a trial of its own, and the effect shrinking towards nothing.

367
00:25:38.598 --> 00:25:46.827
<v Tutor>Tight glucose control and early goal-directed therapy followed a similar arc, though they began as single-centre trials rather than subgroups.

368
00:25:46.827 --> 00:25:53.410
<v Tutor>When an examiner asks you to appraise a subgroup finding, this pair is the cleanest modern example you can give.

369
00:25:53.410 --> 00:25:54.562
<v Tutor>What changed?

370
00:25:54.562 --> 00:25:58.182
<v Tutor>The BICAR-ICU subgroup mortality benefit didn't replicate.

371
00:25:58.182 --> 00:26:02.900
<v Tutor>What survives is a reduction in dialysis that may be partly a design artefact.

372
00:26:03.300 --> 00:26:08.248
<v Tutor>What was BICARICU-2's primary outcome, and what was the result?

373
00:26:08.248 --> 00:26:10.060
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

374
00:26:15.460 --> 00:26:17.678
<v Tutor>Ninety-day all-cause mortality.

375
00:26:17.678 --> 00:26:21.375
<v Tutor>Sixty-two point one versus sixty-one point seven percent.

376
00:26:21.375 --> 00:26:22.626
<v Tutor>No difference.

377
00:26:22.626 --> 00:26:25.583
<v Tutor>The guideline sits just behind the evidence.

378
00:26:25.583 --> 00:26:33.830
<v Tutor>The Surviving Sepsis Campaign's 2026 guideline, Prescott and colleagues in Critical Care Medicine, keeps two conditional recommendations.

379
00:26:33.830 --> 00:26:41.223
<v Tutor>It suggests against bicarbonate to improve haemodynamics or reduce vasopressors in hypoperfusion-induced lactic acidaemia.

380
00:26:41.223 --> 00:26:50.550
<v Tutor>And it suggests bicarbonate in septic shock with a pH of seven point two or lower and AKIN stage two or three kidney injury, on very low certainty evidence.

381
00:26:50.550 --> 00:26:52.882
<v Tutor>Here's how to quote it in an SAQ.

382
00:26:52.882 --> 00:27:11.081
<v Tutor>One sentence: bicarbonate in severe metabolic acidaemia doesn't reduce mortality, BICAR-ICU 2018 and BICARICU-2 2025, but may reduce the need for dialysis in patients with acute kidney injury, so I'd consider it at a pH of seven point two or lower with stage two or three kidney injury, while treating the cause.

383
00:27:11.081 --> 00:27:16.199
<v Tutor>And in pyroglutamic or toxic alcohol acidosis, the cause has a specific treatment.

384
00:27:16.199 --> 00:27:17.905
<v Tutor>Bicarbonate buys time.

385
00:27:17.905 --> 00:27:20.180
<v Tutor>It doesn't replace the antidote.

386
00:27:21.580 --> 00:27:25.471
<v Examiner>This is from the 2020 second sitting, question twenty, part two.

387
00:27:25.471 --> 00:27:31.901
